<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Corcu Loigde</h1><p class="st-meta">400 CE – 1200 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Kingdom of the Érainn in south-west Cork, ancestral to the O'Driscolls, which tradition remembers as rulers of Munster before the Eóganachta.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Corcu Loigde were a people of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/iverni">Érainn</a>, the old population group of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-munster">Munster</a> linked with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iverni">Iverni</a> of Ptolemy. In Irish tradition they held the kingship of Munster before the rise of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eoganacht-chaisil">Eóganachta of Cashel</a> around the 5th century, and afterwards kept a respected place as a free subject kingdom in south-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-cork">Cork</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Their kingdom covered the coast and hinterland from around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clonakilty">Clonakilty</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltimore-county-cork">Baltimore</a> and inland toward <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skibbereen">Skibbereen</a>. St <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ciaran-of-saighir">Ciarán of Saigir</a>, among the earliest Irish saints, was claimed as one of their kin, and a genealogical text on the Corcu Loigde preserves their lineages. The ruling family became known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odriscoll">Ó hEidirsceoil</a>, later O'Driscoll, with power based on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, farmland and the sea.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>From the 12th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-maccarthy">MacCarthys</a> of Desmond and then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-normans">Anglo-Normans</a> pushed the Corcu Loigde to the coast. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odriscoll">O'Driscolls</a> survived as lords of Collymore around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltimore-county-cork">Baltimore</a> and its islands until the early 17th century.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 400 CE – 1200 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">British Isles</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:67.66%;width:15.92%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>400 CE – 1200 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Irish, later Middle Irish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/ireland">Ireland</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Corcu Loígde, Corca Laoighdhe</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=800&amp;focus=corcu-loigde" aria-label="Corcu Loigde on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4495 348 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Corcu Loigde lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4823 622h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4815 604h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M4815 604h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Ireland. <a href="/atlas?year=800&amp;focus=corcu-loigde">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

